// molis/no-empty-catch: a `catch` clause whose block holds no statement and no comment ("catch {}", "catch (error) { }").
// It reports exactly what the earlier empty-catch counter reported (specs/repository-anti-corruption W1-04): a block with
// a comment is a decision somebody wrote down, a block with any statement handles the error, and a promise's
// `.catch(() => {})` is a callback, not a catch clause. Biome's own noEmptyBlockStatements is not used for this because it
// also reports every empty function, which is a different thing (481 sites against 4).
// The statement list is empty and the text of the block is only braces and whitespace: comments are trivia in Biome's
// syntax tree, so the text test is what tells `{ /* why */ }` apart from `{ }`.
language js(typescript);

JsCatchClause(body = $block) as $clause where {
  $block <: JsBlockStatement(statements = []),
  $block <: r"\{\s*\}",
  register_diagnostic(
    span = $clause,
    message = "molis/no-empty-catch: handle the error, or say in a comment inside the block why ignoring it is safe.",
    severity = "error"
  )
}
